  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/3/35/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_56.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Giant-Man enters his lab in the upper floors of a building and decides it's time to offer Jan \"an engagement ring and ask her to marry me!\"&nbsp; But when Jan arrives, she despairs of him ever proposing marriage and decides to make him jealous by announcing that she's off to a party, leaving Hank distraught, pondering over what a fool he's been.&nbsp; She's <i>great</i> as his partner, \"but when it comes to <i>romance</i>, she wants someone as wealthy as she!\"&nbsp; Ah well, perhaps it's all for the best!\n\nBut an observer ant notifies Hank that a strange, suspicious-looking stage magician is planning to perform where Sterling Stuyvesant is hosting the party -- the very same one which Jan is attending!&nbsp; <i>Oh no!</i>  There's not one moment to lose!\n\nAt the party, Jan's friend (and host), Sterling Stuyvesant, makes moves on Jan, not at all being subtle about the way he feels about her, and when she tells him to wait until after the party, he finally introduces \"the Magician,\" who immediately begins to entertain the crowd by performing seemingly harmless feats of magic, as expected.\n\nBut it doesn't take long for his real motive to manifest itself, that of using his magic to rob the party-goers and take their valuables.&nbsp; Jan immediately shrinks down in order to confront him as the Wasp, but the Magician uses his suction wand to pull her inside and trap her!&nbsp; To him, this is even better than jewels!&nbsp; He has \"the famous Wasp\" as his captive and hastily makes his exit.\n\nHank arrives too late and is forced to return to his lab in order to try and find Jan.&nbsp; But he soon finds that even his trusty ants can't locate the Magician.\n\nUnwilling to wait it out, Hank decides to lure the Magician into a trap, so he stages a society party aboard a yacht named \"Neptune,\" hoping that the assembled (and wealthy) guests will be too much of a temptation for the villain.&nbsp; And while the party <i>does</i> eventually take place, Hank is careful to move the guests to another yacht, leaving himself alone on the <i>Neptune</i>, awaiting the arrival of the Magician.&nbsp; He arranges the ship to make it <i>look</i> like there's a major party going on, and sure enough the Magician arrives, only to find Giant-Man waiting for him.\n\nThe Magician flees back to his hovering blimp, which is painted blue in order to blend in with the sky.&nbsp; But because of a nearby capsizing boat, Giant-Man is temporarily distracted and unable to pursue.&nbsp; Yet his proximity to where the Wasp is being held allows her to communicate with him via her cybernetic helmet, allowing Giant-Man to follow the blimp.\n\nHank lassos the flying blimp as Giant-Man, then becomes Ant-Man to avoid dragging it down, allowing him to climb aboard.&nbsp; He frees the Wasp, due to him retaining his normal strength even while reduced in size.&nbsp; Wasp then goes to the blimp's release valve and deflates the huge ship as the now-enlarged Giant-Man confronts the Magician.\n\nThe blimp goes down, and Jan temporarily thinks she's lost Hank forever.&nbsp; But, of course not -- Hank appears, and she declares \"there can <i>never be anyone else for me!</i>\"&nbsp; To which Hank replies \"There never <i>will</i> be, Jan.&nbsp; I promise you that!\"\n\nThe Magician is picked up in the water, hauled off to jail, and the day is saved...",
  "grade_one_liner": "A story that hints of genuine promise in the Hank/Jan relationship morphs horribly into an object lesson in how to squander a perfectly good premise.",
  "subtitle": "Engagement Ring Not Included",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | EngagementRing | width=15%] In the opening moments, it looks like the book might have a flicker of life, as we learn that Hank has finally gathered up the courage to ask Jan to marry him.&nbsp; While that's a [SPAN: severity=success; text=positive sign for the book,] one still is forced to wonder:&nbsp; Given all of Jan's very <i>overt</i> expressions of affection for Hank over all this time, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=what exactly was he worried about?]&nbsp; I mean, the lady has not been <i>subtle</i> at all!\n[BR]\n[BR] But on the other hand... Hank is also a nerdy scientist, so it's fair to cut him a break...\n\n[ASSET: right | MakeHimJealous | width=11%] The drama of the romance at the beginning might have been a step forward in their relationship, but naturally it was turned on its ear when Jan used the excuse of a party invitation with the possibility of a marriage proposal to try and make Hank jealous enough to finally <i>act</i>, not realizing it would have exactly the <i>opposite</i> effect, leaving Hank feeling as if he is unworthy of someone with her social status.\n\nCutting Hank a break is <i>one thing</i>, but this sudden reversal smacks of Stan Lee's misguided attempt to stir the pot, to give readers hope of something actually developing in their relationship, only to snatch it back with a smirk and apology of \"just kidding!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=I was not amused!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIt was a maneuver worthy of just about every issue of <i>Fantastic Four</i>, where Stan and Jack essentially fill around three pages with fluff before anything happens, and per the many tenets laid out in the [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Comic Book Convenience 101] course guide, it was time to ensure that there would be some menace to threaten the title heroes at the very party that Jan was attending.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=In this, at least, the story does not disappoint...]\n\n[ASSET: left | UnearnedCloseness | width=17%] And yet, it gets another strike against it for presenting this heretofore unknown person of Sterling Stuyvesant and putting him on the verge of a <i>marriage proposal</i> to Jan.\n[BR]\n[BR] Such a blatant act could not have made it clearer to readers that this character was of the one-and-done variety, conjured out of left field to be a foil for the [SPAN: severity=error; text=false promise of advancement in the Hank/Jan relationship.]\n\n[HANG0] Sterling shows up and the text suggests they have been close, and yet, as this is his first-ever appearance,\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; text=it has not <i>earned</i> any of this!]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Unbelievable!]\n\n[HANG0] [SPAN: severity=info; text=I was only on page 5, we hadn't even met this month's villain,]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=and already, I could not <i>wait</i> for the magazine to be over!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=This is plot malfeasance of the highest order!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | WaspCaptured | width=10%] [ASSET: right | TrappedInAGlassJar | width=9%] When the Magician finally arrives on page 6, the story refers to him as a \"stage magician,\" which I found eminently appropriate, because he was nothing more than a prop -- an excuse for there to be action in this month's adventure.\n[BR]\n[BR] I almost didn't care about what happened next.\n[BR] <b>CORRECTION:</b>  I <i>really</i> didn't...\n\nWhat followed was... to say the <i>least</i>... an overly-elaborate setup.\n\nHank is able to rent a huge yacht and throw a social event in <i>record time</i>, all for the sake of a trap that comes together with suspicious ease, and when the Magician arrives to prey upon the wealthy guests, he finds he's been <i>tricked</i>!\n[ASSET: center | Tricked | width=large]\n\n[ASSET: left | DayIsSaved | width=13%] His blimp is discovered, Hank frees Jan, who deflates the flying blimp.\n[BR] Hank survives the fall, the Magician is rounded up, and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]\n[BR]\n[BR] (Which, by the way, frees the reader up to do other things... <i>surely</i> a blessing!)\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | AntChariot | width=23%] Before I close, I must reiterate how \"pre-fab\" Ant/Giant-Man feels, with Stan and company trying ever-harder to position Hank as some kind of genius inventor who has a \"completely equipped lab\" (per page 10) and access to gadgets of all varieties.\n[BR]\n[BR] Last month, readers were shown the [SPAN: severity=info; text=Flagpole Skyhook], which the splash page of <i>this</i> issue briefly features, but that absurdity apparently wasn't good enough, because <i>this</i> month features the debut of Hank's handy-dandy [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Flying Ant Chariot] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(patent pending)].\n\nFar from being clever, however, this latest brainstorm comes across as just one more [SPAN: severity=error; text=dumb idea] (and looks even stupider than the FF's flying bathtub).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=Is it any wonder this egghead scientist can't summon the nerve to propose?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(What a doofus!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nFinally, I can't help but wonder why it is that Hank's normal strength was clearly enough to free the Wasp from her small glass prison, but that she didn't even seem to <i>try</i>, unless it's the case that she doesn't share this same ability that Hank has when he's ant-sized.\n\nI can think of no other explanation for why she didn't do <b><i>exactly what Hank did</i></b> and simply tear open her prison.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis was about as barf-inducing a plot as ever for this magazine.&nbsp; The villain was so lame, I found I didn't even <i>care</i> as I trudged through the pages and found I simply could not <i>wait</i> for the story to be over.\n\nAnd even though the two heroes seem to realize at the end how each feels for the other, I'd bet anything that we won't be seeing that engagement ring again!&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: <i>Certainly not</i>, as we know Hank and Jan don't tie the knot until <i>Avengers</i> #60, which is still some years away.]&nbsp; And I doubt we'll be hearing much from Sterling Stuyvesant either!\n\nI could be wrong, of course, but... [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=I'll wager I'm <i>not</i>!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=I struggle to understand how this magazine stayed in publication.]\n\nGiant-Man and the Wasp are part of the Avengers, <i>sure</i>, so that was bound to help some, but their solo adventures so far have been about as bad as they come, and I find myself dreading the days when I'm forced to wade through another issue of <i>Tales to Astonish</i> (which more accurately should be named [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Tales to Abolish!])\n\nAs bad as this issue was, it isn't even the <i>worst</i> the magazine has offered (<i>that</i> would be 1962's [LINK:1962:TTA_39_ant0|TTA #39]), but it ranks right up there with it.\n\n[HANG0] I knew going into this project that there would be days like this...\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=...but I'm not sure how much more of this I can take!]",
  "side_note": [
    "Multiple times on page 15, Giant-Man and the Wasp refer to each other by their real names, right in front of the Magician.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=I thought their identities were supposed to be <i>secret</i>!]",
    "<b>The Wasp Tells a Tale:</b>  \"Beware the Bog Beast!\"\n(by Stan Lee, Larry Lieber and Paul Reinman)\n\nThe Wasp distracts Giant-Man during a workout by posing a riddle.\n\nOn a far-off world, a princess falls in love with a poor peasant (Lorenzo), and her brother, the ruler, swears to get rid of the interloper by coming up with a contest:&nbsp; only the one who can vanquish the Bog Beast shall have his sister's hand in marriage!\n\nOnly three men are brave enough to even try, including Lorenzo, who is content to allow the other two stronger men go first.&nbsp; But neither the strong man or the warrior has any luck.\n\nBut the Wasp refuses to tell Giant-Man how Lorenzo won the right to marry the princess -- unless Hank takes her out to dinner!&nbsp; It's blackmail, but Hank agrees.&nbsp; Only then does she explain that \"lazy\" Lorenzo did nothing at all -- he simply told the princess to cross a bridge that the bog beast was too heavy to follow across.&nbsp; Mission accomplished!\n\nHank is disgusted -- he'd rather face a super-villain than another of Jan's riddles!"
  ],
  "highlight": "A parade of unearned developments leaves the Magician as merely Hank and Jan's third-biggest problem."
}